Jorgen Grahn wrote:
> Ray wrote:
>> Eladar wrote:
>
>>> When I asked just what nethack was coded in, i got the reply "archaic
>>> and badly written C".
>>
>> It's written in C, using the paradigms and conventions of 1980 or 
>> thereabouts.  I would not call it badly written.  On the contrary 
>> its style is quite good according to the standards in use when it 
>> was written.  Current standards are not really better, just 
>> different.  

In 1980 I switched from Fortran developer to C developer and also
started doing sysadmin work.  In 1986 I switched to full time sysadmin. 
The large majority of C coding I've done in my career has been with K&R
pre-ANSI C and in the standard style of the early 1980s.  That explains
why I have always thought that NetHack is well coded not badly coded.  I
can read it like a magazine article.

>> Current coding standards have to be understood as absolutes that 
>> prevent bad uses of certain things by forbidding the thing entirely.
>
> You have a point, but you make it sound as if we're now in some Lawful
> (in the Nethack sense) era. There *are* rigid standards around today,
> there were rigid standards around then, and both now and then they
> are/were ignored by smart, young hackers.  It's not as if AT&T
> developed Nethack as a multi-M$ project ...

But what standards that were current in 1980 are violated by NetHack?  I
know of none.  It's more careful than the code I wrote in that era and I
understand there are still a few folks using our old code.

>> A lot of its functions are longer than you'd currently allow 
>> functions to get.

Which was not a standard in 1980 and therefore not relevant to judging
based on that context.

>> This is for two reasons.  First, stack space 
>> was precious at the time and compilers didn't do auto-inlining 
>> of small functions.
>
> Citation needed -- or are you in the original Dev Team?

I'll offer a different option.  Today any yahoo can start coding.  In
1980 there was an expectation that anyone doing it have a degree in it
and had several years of apprenticeship to filter out the bottom 75% of
those who started.  Error rates grow geometrically with module size and
the solution to that fact has changed from filtering out those who could
not hack it to decreasing the module size so anyone can build one. 
Context matters and with NetHack the context is that time.

Could NetHack be recoded from scratch using today's standards? 
Absolutely and it would probably be a reliable game in the third
release.  Sure enough the current version is still building on the third
release using that previous set of standards.

Tinkering with NetHack code is like a modern mechanic tinkering with a
high pressure steam engine.  Lots of old rules that are unfamiliar, but
don't make the mistake of thinking it's poorly constructed.


